<hr>
<h2>Collapsible Sections</h2>
<p>Use <code>:::details</code> with a required <code>summary</code>. The content is regular Markdown, and details can be nested:</p>
<details class="mdrs-details">
 <summary class="mdrs-details-summary">
 <span class="mdrs-details-title">What does server-side rendered math actually mean?</span>
 <span class="mdrs-details-icon" aria-hidden="true">
 <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="mdrs-icon mdrs-icon-chevrons-up-down"><path d="m7 15 5 5 5-5"/><path d="m7 9 5-5 5 5"/></svg>
 <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="mdrs-icon mdrs-icon-chevrons-down-up"><path d="m7 20 5-5 5 5"/><path d="m7 4 5 5 5-5"/></svg>
 </span>
 </summary>
 <div class="mdrs-details-body">
 <p>KaTeX compiles LaTeX expressions into HTML and MathML markup during the SSR pass on the server. The browser receives fully-formed math output: no JavaScript bundle, no render flash, no layout shift. This is why formulas on this page appear instantly even with JavaScript disabled.</p>
 </div>
</details><details class="mdrs-details">
 <summary class="mdrs-details-summary">
 <span class="mdrs-details-title">How are Mermaid diagrams loaded without blocking the page?</span>
 <span class="mdrs-details-icon" aria-hidden="true">
 <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="mdrs-icon mdrs-icon-chevrons-up-down"><path d="m7 15 5 5 5-5"/><path d="m7 9 5-5 5 5"/></svg>
 <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="mdrs-icon mdrs-icon-chevrons-down-up"><path d="m7 20 5-5 5 5"/><path d="m7 4 5 5 5-5"/></svg>
 </span>
 </summary>
 <div class="mdrs-details-body">
 <p>The markdown compiler replaces each mermaid fence with a lightweight placeholder div. A client-side observer watches for viewport intersection, then dynamically imports the Mermaid library and renders the SVG in place. The diagram never blocks initial page load.</p>
<details class="mdrs-details">
 <summary class="mdrs-details-summary">
 <span class="mdrs-details-title">Nested example</span>
 <span class="mdrs-details-icon" aria-hidden="true">
 <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="mdrs-icon mdrs-icon-chevrons-up-down"><path d="m7 15 5 5 5-5"/><path d="m7 9 5-5 5 5"/></svg>
 <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="mdrs-icon mdrs-icon-chevrons-down-up"><path d="m7 20 5-5 5 5"/><path d="m7 4 5 5 5-5"/></svg>
 </span>
 </summary>
 <div class="mdrs-details-body">
 <p>Details can contain any other block, including code and lists:</p>
<ul class="mdrs-list mdrs-list--unordered">
<li class="mdrs-list-item"><span class="mdrs-list-marker" aria-hidden="true"></span><div class="mdrs-list-content">A list item</div></li>
<li class="mdrs-list-item"><span class="mdrs-list-marker" aria-hidden="true"></span><div class="mdrs-list-content">Another list item</div></li>
</ul>
<pre data-mdrs-filename="nested.sh"><code class="language-bash">echo &quot;inside a nested details block&quot;
</code></pre>
 </div>
</details>
 </div>
</details><hr>
<h2>Footnotes</h2>
<p>The engine compiles <code>[^label]</code> markers into superscript links with numbered back-references<sup><a id="mdrs-fn-ref-1" href="#mdrs-fn-1" data-mdrs-fn-ref="" aria-describedby="mdrs-fn-label">[1]</a></sup>. KaTeX math runs entirely at build time<sup><a id="mdrs-fn-ref-math" href="#mdrs-fn-math" data-mdrs-fn-ref="" aria-describedby="mdrs-fn-label">[2]</a></sup>, and the <code>api.v1.*</code> resolver demonstrated above fetches data from the same database that powers the REST API<sup><a id="mdrs-fn-ref-api" href="#mdrs-fn-api" data-mdrs-fn-ref="" aria-describedby="mdrs-fn-label">[3]</a></sup>.</p>
<section class="mdrs-footnotes mdrs-references" data-mdrs-footnotes="">
<h2 id="mdrs-fn-label">References</h2>
<ol>
<li id="mdrs-fn-1">
<p><a href="#mdrs-fn-ref-1" class="mdrs-footnote-backref" aria-label="Back to reference">^</a>Directives like <code>:::define</code>, <code>:::tabs</code>, and <code>:::callout</code> are parsed in a pre-processing pass over raw markdown before <code>marked.parse()</code> runs.</p>
</li>
<li id="mdrs-fn-math">
<p><a href="#mdrs-fn-ref-math" class="mdrs-footnote-backref" aria-label="Back to reference">^</a>KaTeX renders <strong>server-side during SSR</strong>, so no client JavaScript is required to display formulas. Mermaid diagrams, by contrast, hydrate lazily in the browser.</p>
</li>
<li id="mdrs-fn-api">
<p><a href="#mdrs-fn-ref-api" class="mdrs-footnote-backref" aria-label="Back to reference">^</a>The <code>api.v1.*</code> resolver calls the same data queries as <code>GET /api/v1/author</code> and <code>GET /api/v1/blog</code>, but at compile time rather than via HTTP.</p>
</li>
</ol>
</section>

"
`;
